WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.300
 So let's learn base sixteen, otherwise known as hexadecimal.

00:00:04.300 --> 00:00:10.900
 So the key to, it would be nice if we could just use base two for everything, but zeros and ones are easy to understand,

00:00:10.900 --> 00:00:19.300
 but when numbers get larger, like characters for example are eight bits, it's really hard to represent that all in base two.

00:00:19.300 --> 00:00:26.700
 So we tend to see in the real world that most machine language and other things require you to understand hexadecimal.

00:00:26.700 --> 00:00:35.300
 So if you take a look at base two, it was easy. We had a simple conversion table, and I hope by now some of you have memorized this little table.

00:00:35.300 --> 00:00:41.700
 I know I've memorized. I just, you know, when I see seven, I see three ones. When I see six, I see one, one, zero.

00:00:41.700 --> 00:00:49.000
 I know this by heart, okay? Now, that's cool. We're seeing all numbers that we're familiar with.

00:00:49.000 --> 00:00:56.800
 Base sixteen says we are going to represent zero through fifteen with a single digit, right?

00:00:56.800 --> 00:01:03.700
 So fifteen is fifteen, but with two digits, and so the whole idea of changing bases is that we have digits.

00:01:03.700 --> 00:01:11.500
 So we need to come up with a single digit that represents nine, ten, eleven, twelve, thirteen, fourteen, and fifteen.

00:01:11.500 --> 00:01:20.100
 And so I basically said, okay, wouldn't it be cool if we would use emojis for that, because I have trouble remembering hexadecimal.

00:01:20.100 --> 00:01:33.600
 So if we imagine that ten, which is kind of zero, eleven, twelve, thirteen, they don't have an emoji for four fingers, and then fifteen.

00:01:33.600 --> 00:01:43.000
 So I put a shamrock in for fourteen, and so the point here is you need a single character to represent it.

00:01:43.000 --> 00:01:51.800
 And so I made this up, and then I'm like, hey, AI, what emojis would you use to represent ten through fifteen?

00:01:51.800 --> 00:01:58.900
 So there is an actual emoji that's ten, but there is no emoji for eleven, and so they use the pause emoji.

00:01:58.900 --> 00:02:06.900
 And then they used a clock, twelve o'clock, one o'clock, two o'clock, and three o'clock, and it's not too bad.

00:02:06.900 --> 00:02:15.500
 And American Sign Languages uses one hand, I think this is eleven, or maybe, yeah, I think this is eleven.

00:02:15.500 --> 00:02:18.300
 And so British Sign Language is different.

00:02:18.300 --> 00:02:25.300
 The problem is we don't get motion when we make emojis, so we can't really use American Sign Language,

00:02:25.300 --> 00:02:32.500
 even though American Sign Language has way to represent eleven, twelve, thirteen, fourteen, and fifteen with kind of a gesture.

00:02:32.500 --> 00:02:42.500
 So the problem was, is basically whatever, 1930, 1940, when we started using hexadecimal, we didn't have emojis, it was just too early.

00:02:42.500 --> 00:02:52.700
 And so all we had was ASCII characters, and so we have repurposed A through F to represent ten through fifteen for base sixteen numbers.

00:02:52.700 --> 00:03:04.700
 So A is ten, the first number that is not a single digit, right, and F is fifteen, and F is all ones, four ones is one, one, one, one, one, is F.

00:03:04.700 --> 00:03:12.700
 Okay, so we just used A through F. It's a little weird, because there's the letter A, and then there is the hexadecimal digit A,

00:03:12.700 --> 00:03:19.400
 but you have to kind of get used to it and say, oh, we're in the context of a number here, so that means ten, A means ten.

00:03:19.400 --> 00:03:27.100
 So if we look at the conversion between base sixteen and base ten, and I've got my little cheat sheet here of ten through fifteen,

00:03:27.100 --> 00:03:37.200
 because zero through nine is pretty easy. Again, base ten says that each column is a power of ten, so the far right column is ten to the zero, which is the ones column.

00:03:37.200 --> 00:03:44.400
 The middle column is ten to the one, which is the tens column, and then the third column is ten squared, which is the hundreds column.

00:03:44.400 --> 00:03:54.400
 So a hundred and twenty-three is one times a hundred, two times ten, plus three times one, which is one twenty-three base ten, which of course it is.

00:03:54.400 --> 00:04:00.300
 In base sixteen now, we have more digits to work with. So we have seven be base sixteen.

00:04:00.300 --> 00:04:09.900
 Well, the far right is sixteen to the zero, or one, and then the second digit is sixteen to the first power.

00:04:09.900 --> 00:04:19.700
 So the result is seven times sixteen plus eleven times one, which is one hundred and twelve plus eleven, which is one hundred and twenty-three.

00:04:19.700 --> 00:04:26.300
 Now, one thing we like about base sixteen is it's easily converted to and from base two.

00:04:26.300 --> 00:04:32.700
 And so if you have eight bit numbers, you can represent them with two base sixteen digits.

00:04:32.700 --> 00:04:36.100
 And that's what we're going to work with a lot going forward is eight bit numbers.

00:04:36.100 --> 00:04:42.300
 So you can take and convert like seven be base sixteen, which is a byte.

00:04:42.300 --> 00:04:47.100
 And you can convert the seven and then you convert the B and that's the base two.

00:04:47.100 --> 00:04:58.300
 And so to some degree, hexadecimal is just a more succinct base two, where four digits of base two become one digit of base sixteen.

00:04:58.300 --> 00:05:04.500
 If we did this on base ten, there wouldn't be this nice lineup because you can make really long hexadecimal numbers.

00:05:04.500 --> 00:05:09.400
 And they still line up on every four bit boundary with base two.

00:05:09.400 --> 00:05:16.900
 So for now, keep it simple. I tend to have to go through base ten.

00:05:16.900 --> 00:05:19.100
 You know, we have eight's column now.

00:05:19.100 --> 00:05:27.700
 So the top line is one eight plus one two, which is ten, eight plus two is ten, and that is a base sixteen.

00:05:27.700 --> 00:05:35.300
 Eight, which is one zero zero zero is eight base ten and eight base sixteen. So all the numbers zero through nine are easy.

00:05:35.300 --> 00:05:41.300
 And then all ones one one one one is eight plus four plus two plus one, which is fifteen.

00:05:41.300 --> 00:05:43.500
 And that's F and base sixteen.

00:05:43.500 --> 00:05:49.800
 So I've told you that I'm pretty much a whiz at base eight, which is three base two digits.

00:05:49.800 --> 00:05:55.000
 I can kind of do that in my sleep. I am not very good at base sixteen.

00:05:55.000 --> 00:05:57.800
 Hopefully you'll be better at it or maybe not.

00:05:57.800 --> 00:06:03.600
 So this is my this is how I do base sixteen in my head and it's not pretty.

00:06:03.600 --> 00:06:07.300
 So F is easy because that's fifteen.

00:06:07.300 --> 00:06:11.400
 That's the largest base sixteen digit and it's all ones. Right.

00:06:11.400 --> 00:06:15.200
 That's cool. It's base sixteen. F is one less. It's all ones.

00:06:15.200 --> 00:06:19.000
 If you add one to it, it's it turns into the sixteen column.

00:06:19.000 --> 00:06:25.200
 So an E I can do E because I just take the all the digits and I take off the one bit.

00:06:25.200 --> 00:06:27.600
 Right. So that's like fourteen.

00:06:27.600 --> 00:06:32.200
 I can get that. A is easy because it's like nine.

00:06:32.200 --> 00:06:35.500
 What's after nine? Oh, it's A. And so I can do that.

00:06:35.500 --> 00:06:42.400
 So I understand that's ten and B is eleven and is like it's kind of like one beyond A.

00:06:42.400 --> 00:06:47.700
 And so I can see what B is. I have problems with C and D. Right.

00:06:47.700 --> 00:06:54.300
 I mean, I eventually have to slow down and use my brain and go A ten B eleven C twelve.

00:06:54.300 --> 00:06:57.300
 And then I have to convert to base two, which is one one zero.

00:06:57.300 --> 00:06:59.200
 And then I'm like, oh, that's a plus four. Oh, yeah.

00:06:59.200 --> 00:07:03.500
 Twelve. Yeah. There you go. And D just makes me crazy. Right.

00:07:03.500 --> 00:07:09.200
 I sooner or later, sometimes with D, I'm like, oh, what is it?

00:07:09.200 --> 00:07:12.900
 And then I have to write it down. So for a good time,

00:07:12.900 --> 00:07:20.900
 you can go ahead and ask AI what is a good way to do hex to base two conversions in your head.

00:07:20.900 --> 00:07:23.800
 I did that and it was really complex.

00:07:23.800 --> 00:07:27.000
 So no. So maybe I'm OK by being confused about this.

00:07:27.000 --> 00:07:33.500
 So just make yourself a little cheat sheet whenever you have to do hex or just get used to it or look at a table.

00:07:33.500 --> 00:07:40.200
 A lot of what we're going to do is represent characters and characters are asking characters are eight bits

00:07:40.200 --> 00:07:44.800
 and can really nicely and densely be represented by two hex digits.

00:07:44.800 --> 00:07:48.000
 We call these half bytes nibbles.

00:07:48.000 --> 00:07:54.500
 Not that it matters much, but this ASCII chart shows the decimal, the hex

00:07:54.500 --> 00:07:57.000
 octal, which we tend not to use, and then the binary.

00:07:57.000 --> 00:08:00.700
 So if you look at forty two, it's kind of small on the screen.

00:08:00.700 --> 00:08:08.100
 But you see that it's forty two decimal and zero X to a hexadecimal, which is asterisk.

00:08:08.100 --> 00:08:12.900
 And so I tend to just look these things up and I don't really convert them

00:08:12.900 --> 00:08:17.900
 and just use hex as a way to look things up in things like ASCII tables.

00:08:17.900 --> 00:08:26.100
 Now, while we're speaking about ASCII, so ASCII is a historical code set,

00:08:26.100 --> 00:08:32.500
 basically, that is very Western and really goes back to Latin, really.

00:08:32.500 --> 00:08:39.800
 And because we have eight bit bytes and the West built computers early on,

00:08:39.800 --> 00:08:43.800
 we tended to have ASCII character sets were universal across computers.

00:08:43.800 --> 00:08:45.500
 But that's not real.

00:08:45.500 --> 00:08:51.700
 There's Asian character sets and Indian character sets and Arabic and Persian character sets.

00:08:51.700 --> 00:08:54.000
 They're they're all kind of different.

00:08:54.000 --> 00:09:01.400
 And so what happened was in in the time where computing became a world wide activity,

00:09:01.400 --> 00:09:08.200
 we needed to come up with a way to interchange characters that are way bigger than eight bits.

00:09:08.200 --> 00:09:11.400
 And somebody came up with the idea of Unicode.

00:09:11.400 --> 00:09:17.900
 And Unicode has all kinds of character sets, you know, Latin and ASCII start.

00:09:17.900 --> 00:09:20.400
 And then they just have all kinds of character sets.

00:09:20.400 --> 00:09:24.500
 Some of these are Latin characters with like umlauts and all things like that.

00:09:24.500 --> 00:09:28.200
 And some of them are like Asian characters that are pictograms.

00:09:28.200 --> 00:09:31.400
 Now, recently, emojis are in Unicode as well.

00:09:31.400 --> 00:09:36.200
 And so some of the emojis that I've showed you are actually have a Unicode code.

00:09:36.200 --> 00:09:40.400
 Unicode represents things by 32 bits.

00:09:40.400 --> 00:09:45.900
 That's pretty large, given that eight bits is how ASCII is represented in Unicode is 32 bits.

00:09:45.900 --> 00:09:51.600
 But the idea is, is there's a lot of characters sets around this universe.

00:09:51.600 --> 00:09:54.000
 Now, we're not inventing too many more.

00:09:54.000 --> 00:09:56.800
 And they did things like added emojis to Unicode.

00:09:56.800 --> 00:10:01.600
 But basically, 32 bits is like in the billions of possible characters.

00:10:01.600 --> 00:10:03.500
 So I think we're probably okay.

00:10:03.500 --> 00:10:09.800
 The problem is, is as we move between computers or store data on hard drives,

00:10:09.800 --> 00:10:13.000
 we don't really want every character to take up 32 bits.

00:10:13.000 --> 00:10:18.000
 So for example, Python 3 internally represents strings as Unicode,

00:10:18.000 --> 00:10:20.400
 which means they're pretty large.

00:10:20.400 --> 00:10:25.300
 Okay, but then before we stick them on a hard drive or send them across a network,

00:10:25.300 --> 00:10:27.000
 we condense them.

00:10:27.000 --> 00:10:34.900
 And there are ways to compress these 32-bit Unicodes into shorter.

00:10:34.900 --> 00:10:38.100
 So one is UTF-32, which is four-byte stuff.

00:10:38.100 --> 00:10:38.900
 That's the long one.

00:10:38.900 --> 00:10:40.200
 It's fixed length.

00:10:40.200 --> 00:10:43.800
 And it just says we'll take that whole Unicode and send it along.

00:10:43.800 --> 00:10:46.000
 UTF-16 is fixed length.

00:10:46.000 --> 00:10:47.200
 It's two bytes.

00:10:47.200 --> 00:10:49.100
 It's a lot denser.

00:10:49.100 --> 00:10:52.800
 And there's a mapping.

00:10:52.800 --> 00:10:57.000
 But the most successful one of these is a thing called UTF-8,

00:10:57.000 --> 00:10:58.600
 which might not be intuitive.

00:10:58.600 --> 00:11:02.700
 But basically, UTF-8 is one to four bytes.

00:11:02.700 --> 00:11:08.900
 And it is for most ASCII, UTF-8 and ASCII are the same things.

00:11:08.900 --> 00:11:11.900
 And then there's these little extension codes.

00:11:11.900 --> 00:11:15.100
 I mean, this is a 16-bit code or a 32-bit code.

00:11:15.100 --> 00:11:19.800
 And so you can represent all of Unicode with UTF-8.

00:11:19.800 --> 00:11:23.400
 Sometimes you're sending one byte if it's ASCII, sometimes two,

00:11:23.400 --> 00:11:26.300
 three, and even four bytes are being sent across.

00:11:26.300 --> 00:11:30.900
 And so UTF-8 is pretty much the practice for encoding data

00:11:30.900 --> 00:11:32.900
 to be exchanged across systems.

00:11:32.900 --> 00:11:37.600
 So I've got to have a graph here that sort of shows that in 2008,

00:11:37.600 --> 00:11:39.900
 there was choices to be made.

00:11:39.900 --> 00:11:44.300
 But since then, everything is pretty much UTF-8.

00:11:44.300 --> 00:11:48.600
 And so the point now where systems that are reading and writing data

00:11:48.600 --> 00:11:52.200
 to disk or the network only pretty much use UTF-8.

00:11:52.200 --> 00:11:54.700
 And it happens to be ASCII, that works okay.

00:11:54.700 --> 00:11:58.500
 But what we're doing is we're working on a computer from the 1970s,

00:11:58.500 --> 00:12:01.300
 which is the 6502 computer.

00:12:01.300 --> 00:12:05.200
 And so we're going to just accept that it's ASCII for now.

00:12:05.200 --> 00:12:10.000
 But real computers tend to work in UTF-8 and can convert from ASCII

00:12:10.000 --> 00:12:13.400
 or Unicode to UTF-8 quite nicely.

00:12:13.400 --> 00:12:23.400
 [ Music ]

